  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Partial fit. The two-slot envelope keeps payload opaque behind a pCID boundary and requires unknown-handler results to stay unsupported and unverified, which helps mixed-version forwarding without hidden global state. But it is still a wire-envelope draft, not a full CID-codec or CAS type-binding scheme, so opaque storage, advertisement, and strict safe-salvage rules are only partly covered.",
    "strengths": [
      "Clear envelope/payload split lets peers retain and relay bytes without semantic parsing.",
      "Unknown pCID behavior is explicit and visibly non-authoritative rather than silently interpreted.",
      "Deterministic CBOR plus wrapper-based protocol selection is plausible and evolution-friendly."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The scenario is about unknown CID codecs, while the spec defines unknown pCID handling at the envelope layer.",
      "Advertisement and re-publication behavior for unknown objects is not specified.",
      "Wrapper-only signature/authorship handling adds indirection for audit and policy checks."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Best-effort salvage tooling could drift into unsafe or misleading parsing in practice.",
      "Different implementations may diverge on what opaque forwarding or inspection is acceptable.",
      "Draft status and unfrozen pCID/spec handling weaken long-horizon interoperability confidence."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "How should unknown CID-codec objects be stored and re-advertised bit-for-bit without decoding?",
      "What minimum safe behavior is mandatory for best-effort tooling beyond raw byte retention?",
      "How are local promise-accounting and provenance records attached when integrity evidence lives in wrappers?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
